{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310815,"nonce":"wPQ/TtjTMrscSmBaNkHXdXu10OU=","title":"2026-06-08 wasm parity session — migrated haxe.io.Bytes from host-side Vec\u003cu8\u003e to wasm linear memory + 4 GiB cap; unblocks all 147 Llama ...","message":"Arc landed at commit 34d74c8 (2026-06-08).\n\n**Why:** The wasm path through `rayzor run --wasm` had a silent ABI\nmismatch between the wasm runner and the runtime-wasm cdylib. The runner\nreturned small-int handles into a host-side `bytes_handles: BTreeMap\u003ci32,\nVec\u003cu8\u003e\u003e`; runtime-wasm functions like `qtensor_from_bytes_q6_k` do\n`bytes_slice(handle, needed)` which dereferences the handle as\n`*const HaxeBytes` in wasm linear memory. The F32 path (`Tensor.fromBytesF32`)\nsilently stored null Tensors; Q6_K's explicit null check on\n`QTensor.fromBytesQ6K` surfaced the bug at the third GGUF tensor.\n\n**How to apply:** All `haxe.io.Bytes` handles in the wasm runner are now\n40-byte `HaxeBytes` struct pointers (ptr/len/cap at offsets 0/4/8,\ntrailing kind/owner/refcount/fd fields zeroed) in wasm linear memory.\n`sub` and `subWithBase` allocate header-only views — no data copy. All\n22 `haxe_bytes_*` ops + `haxe_file_get_bytes` migrated with a dual-mode\nread/write helper that falls back to the legacy `bytes_handles` map for\nbackward compat. `host_alloc` grows the imported `SharedMemory` on demand\n(delta = size + 16 MiB slack) when an allocation would underflow past\na 4 MiB app-heap reserve. SharedMemory + runtime-wasm cdylib max bumped\n1 GiB → 4 GiB (wasm32 cap, 65536 pages) so a 770 MB GGUF + per-weight\nQTensor copies all fit.\n\n**Result:** First wasm decode of the full Llama 3.2 1B Q4_K_M file.\nAll 147 GGUF tensors (F32, F16, Q4_K_M, Q6_K) load successfully. Trap\nrelocates to a separate ABI bug downstream — see\n[[bugs_wasm_anon_struct_return_zeroed]] for the next blocker.\n\nTwo side bugs uncovered:\n- [[bugs_wasm_std_int_large_float]] — `Std.int(Float)` returns 0 on wasm\n  for nElem ≳ 16M. Worked around in `GGUFReader.blockedSize` with\n  `nElem \u003e\u003e 8` on the GGUF block-256 path.\n- `unbox_int_from_memory` mis-interprets a primitive i32 that happens\n  to be \u003e 65536 and 4-byte aligned (e.g. a 215 MB tensor length) as a\n  Dynamic pointer, dereferencing into the GGUF data region. Fixed by\n  skipping unbox on known-primitive params in `sub_base_u64lh`. A\n  longer-term fix would tighten the unbox heuristic so random GGUF\n  bytes can't match `type_id ∈ {2,3} + valid value_ptr`.\n\nSupersedes [[project_wasm_phase2]] — wasmtime now runs through the\nsingle-threaded load path. Phase 6 threading still pending.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_wasm_bytes_in_linear_memory_arc` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}